{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213431",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,1)$ requires that $(1,1)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,3)$ requires that $(1,3)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,3)$ with $(3,2)$ requires that $(1,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,1)$ whose second component is $1.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $1:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,1)$ with $(1,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nWe can stop checking since we have found a chain of pairs that do not satisfy the transitivity condition. Therefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(2,1)\\in R$ and $(1,2)\\in R$ but $(2,2)\\notin R,$ the relation is  $\\text{not transitive}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
